## What the experiment says as it runs: its steps, board and answer. These texts come with the experiment when it opens; its card is in depth-pressure.ftl.

depth-pressure-command-apply = There is no vessel of liquid.
depth-pressure-command-apply-2 = The funnel goes into the liquid, no deeper than the bottom.
depth-pressure-labdepth-say = Keeping the depth ({ $depth } cm), turn the funnel with its film sideways. What will the manometer show?
depth-pressure-labdepth-button = ↪ { $depth } cm, film sideways
depth-pressure-labdepth-plan = turn the funnel sideways at { $depth } cm
depth-pressure-labdepth-say-2 = Lower the funnel, film down, to a depth of { $depth } cm. How far apart are the water levels in the manometer?
depth-pressure-labdepth-button-2 = ⬇ To a depth of { $depth } cm
depth-pressure-labdepth-plan-2 = lower the funnel to { $depth } cm
depth-pressure-answer-why = A liquid is heavy: each layer presses on the layers below with the weight of all that is above it. At a depth h each square metre carries a column of liquid h high with a mass ρ·h, that is, a weight ρ·g·h — so the pressure grows exactly in proportion to the depth. And the bits of a liquid slide past each other easily and pass that pressure on in all directions alike (Pascal’s law): the film turned sideways feels the same pressure.
depth-pressure-answer-say = Twice as deep — twice the pressure!
depth-pressure-answer-say-2 = Let us look at the manometer.
depth-pressure-answer-text = {" "}The film turned sideways at { $h } gave the same Δh = { $dh }: at one depth the pressure is the same in all directions.
depth-pressure-answer-text-2 = { $l }. The pressure the manometer shows (p = ρ_water·g·Δh) grew in proportion to the depth: p / h ≈ { $mean } Pa/m everywhere. From it the liquid’s density ρ = p / (g·h) ≈ { $rho } kg/m³ (the table gives { $table } kg/m³).{ $h }
depth-pressure-say = The film sideways at { $d } — and the manometer’s levels differ just the same: by { $v }!
depth-pressure-say-2 = The film { $d } deep: the water levels in the manometer differ by { $v }.
depth-pressure-board-where = , film sideways
depth-pressure-board-title = the pressure at { $h }{ $where }
depth-pressure-board-title-2 = the liquid’s density (from { $h })
